{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09700","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09700","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09700","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Malavika","familyName":"Suresh","name":"Suresh, Malavika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahaf","familyName":"Aljundi","name":"Aljundi, Rahaf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ikechukwu","familyName":"Nkisi-Orji","name":"Nkisi-Orji, Ikechukwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nirmalie","familyName":"Wiratunga","name":"Wiratunga, Nirmalie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross-Layer Attention Probing for Fine-Grained Hallucination Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T14:37:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-16T13:07:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-19T01:05:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the large-scale adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs) in various applications, there is a growing reliability concern due to their tendency to generate inaccurate text, i.e. hallucinations. In this work, we propose Cross-Layer Attention Probing (CLAP), a novel activation probing technique for hallucination detection, which processes the LLM activations across the entire residual stream as a joint sequence. Our empirical evaluations using five LLMs and three tasks show that CLAP improves hallucination detection compared to baselines on both greedy decoded responses as well as responses sampled at higher temperatures, thus enabling fine-grained detection, i.e. the ability to disambiguate hallucinations and non-hallucinations among different sampled responses to a given prompt. This allows us to propose a detect-then-mitigate strategy using CLAP to reduce hallucinations and improve LLM reliability compared to direct mitigation approaches. Finally, we show that CLAP maintains high reliability even when applied out-of-distribution. Code available at: https://github.com/itsmemala/CLAP"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at the TRUST-AI workshop, ECAI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09700","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:35Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-19T02:53:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04183","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04183","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04183","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aishik","familyName":"Mandal","name":"Mandal, Aishik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanmoy","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Tanmoy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iryna","familyName":"Gurevych","name":"Gurevych, Iryna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T12:59:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:39:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-09T10:37:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-12T01:28:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-30T13:58:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:08:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The growing demand for scalable psychological counseling highlights the need for high-quality, privacy-compliant data, yet such data remains scarce. Here we introduce MAGneT, a novel multi-agent framework for synthetic psychological counseling session generation that decomposes counselor response generation into coordinated sub-tasks handled by specialized LLM agents, each modeling a key psychological technique. Unlike prior single-agent approaches, MAGneT better captures the structure and nuance of real counseling. We further propose a unified evaluation framework that consolidates diverse automatic metrics and expands expert assessment from four to nine counseling aspects, thus addressing inconsistencies in prior evaluation protocols. Empirically, MAGneT substantially outperforms existing methods: experts prefer MAGneT-generated sessions in 77.2% of cases on average across the nine aspects over the strongest baseline, and sessions generated by MAGneT using Llama3-8B-Instruct backbone yield 3.2% higher general counseling skills and 4.3% higher CBT-specific skills on cognitive therapy rating scale (CTRS). An open source Llama3-8B-Instruct model fine-tuned on MAGneT-generated data also outperforms models fine-tuned using baseline synthetic datasets by 6.9% on average on CTRS. We make our code, data and fine-tuned model public."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"50 pages, 39 figures, 20 Tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04183","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:41:07Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:41:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:00:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.19305","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.19305","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.19305","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifu","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yifu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongzhe","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Yongzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueqian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xueqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Wavelet Fourier Diffuser: Frequency-Aware Diffusion Model for Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T08:50:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:00:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-02T14:25:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-03T01:02:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion probability models have shown significant promise in offline reinforcement learning by directly modeling trajectory sequences. However, existing approaches primarily focus on time-domain features while overlooking frequency-domain features, leading to frequency shift and degraded performance according to our observation. In this paper, we investigate the RL problem from a new perspective of the frequency domain. We first observe that time-domain-only approaches inadvertently introduce shifts in the low-frequency components of the frequency domain, which results in trajectory instability and degraded performance. To address this issue, we propose Wavelet Fourier Diffuser (WFDiffuser), a novel diffusion-based RL framework that integrates Discrete Wavelet Transform to decompose trajectories into low- and high-frequency components. To further enhance diffusion modeling for each component, WFDiffuser employs Short-Time Fourier Transform and cross attention mechanisms to extract frequency-domain features and facilitate cross-frequency interaction. Extensive experiment results on the D4RL benchmark demonstrate that WFDiffuser effectively mitigates frequency shift, leading to smoother, more stable trajectories and improved decision-making performance over existing methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"IJCNN 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19305","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-25T01:41:55Z","registered":"2025-09-25T01:41:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-03T03:10:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.05367","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.05367","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.05367","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shei Pern","familyName":"Chua","name":"Chua, Shei Pern","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen Leng","familyName":"Thai","name":"Thai, Zhen Leng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai Jun","familyName":"Teh","name":"Teh, Kai Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qibing","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Qibing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiaolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Between a Rock and a Hard Place: The Tension Between Ethical Reasoning and Safety Alignment in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T05:53:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-09T00:01:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T11:05:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:30:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-10T05:42:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:16:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-15T05:09:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:28:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-30T08:23:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-02T00:33:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model safety alignment predominantly operates on a binary assumption that requests are either safe or unsafe. This classification proves insufficient when models encounter ethical dilemmas, where the capacity to reason through moral trade-offs creates a distinct attack surface. We formalize this vulnerability through TRIAL, a multi-turn red-teaming methodology that embeds harmful requests within ethical framings. TRIAL achieves high attack success rates across most tested models by systematically exploiting the model's ethical reasoning capabilities to frame harmful actions as morally necessary compromises. Building on these insights, we introduce ERR (Ethical Reasoning Robustness), a defense framework that distinguishes between instrumental responses that enable harmful outcomes and explanatory responses that analyze ethical frameworks without endorsing harmful acts. ERR employs a Layer-Stratified Harm-Gated LoRA architecture, achieving robust defense against reasoning-based attacks while preserving model utility."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05367","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-09T02:23:52Z","registered":"2025-09-09T02:23:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:52:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04310","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04310","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04310","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunbo","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Yunbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liming","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Liming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Beckenbauer","name":"Beckenbauer, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandra","familyName":"Brintrup","name":"Brintrup, Alexandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EvoEmo: Towards Evolved Emotional Policies for Adversarial LLM Agents in Multi-Turn Price Negotiation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T15:23:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:47:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-09T02:43:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:17:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T16:04:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T02:06:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-26T11:47:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:53:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent research on Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning in Large Language Models (LLMs) has demonstrated that agents can engage in \\textit{complex}, \\textit{multi-turn} negotiations, opening new avenues for agentic AI. However, existing LLM agents largely overlook the functional role of emotions in such negotiations, instead generating passive, preference-driven emotional responses that make them vulnerable to manipulation and strategic exploitation by adversarial counterparts. To address this gap, we present EvoEmo, an evolutionary reinforcement learning framework that optimizes dynamic emotional expression in negotiations. EvoEmo models emotional state transitions as a Markov Decision Process and employs population-based genetic optimization to evolve high-reward emotion policies across diverse negotiation scenarios. We further propose an evaluation framework with two baselines -- vanilla strategies and fixed-emotion strategies -- for benchmarking emotion-aware negotiation. Extensive experiments and ablation studies show that EvoEmo consistently outperforms both baselines, achieving higher success rates, higher efficiency, and increased buyer savings. This findings highlight the importance of adaptive emotional expression in enabling more effective LLM agents for multi-turn negotiation. The code is available at \\href{https://github.com/Yunbo-max/EvoEmo}{\\textcolor{red}{https://github.com/Yunbo-max/EvoEmo}}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04310","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:44:08Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:44:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:35:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04334","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04334","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04334","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyue","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Pengyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharon","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sharon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GeoArena: Evaluating Open-World Geographic Reasoning in Large Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T15:52:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:48:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T15:02:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-08T00:43:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T02:43:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-22T00:24:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-11T15:47:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:40:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-19T14:49:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:15:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Geographic reasoning is a fundamental cognitive capability that requires models to infer plausible locations by synthesizing visual evidence with spatial world knowledge. Despite recent advances in large vision-language models (LVLMs), existing evaluation paradigms remain largely outcome-centric, relying on static datasets and predefined labels that are conceptually misaligned with open-world geographic inference. Such outcome-centric evaluations often focus exclusively on label matching, leaving the underlying linguistic reasoning chains as unexamined black boxes. In this work, we introduce GeoArena, a dynamic, human-preference-based evaluation framework for benchmarking open-world geographic reasoning. GeoArena reframes evaluation as a pairwise reasoning alignment task on in-the-wild images, where human judges compare model-generated explanations based on reasoning quality, evidence synthesis, and plausibility. We deploy GeoArena as a public platform and benchmark 17 frontier LVLMs using thousands of human judgments, which complements existing benchmarks and supports the development of geographically grounded, human-aligned AI systems. We further provide detailed analyses of model behavior, including reliability of human preferences and factors influencing judgments of geographic reasoning quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 Main"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04334","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:44:42Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:44:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:48:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04403","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04403","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04403","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingen","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Jingen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-adaptive Dataset Construction for Real-World Multimodal Safety Scenarios"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T17:13:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:51:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T11:09:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:39:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) are rapidly evolving, presenting increasingly complex safety challenges. However, current dataset construction methods, which are risk-oriented, fail to cover the growing complexity of real-world multimodal safety scenarios (RMS). And due to the lack of a unified evaluation metric, their overall effectiveness remains unproven. This paper introduces a novel image-oriented self-adaptive dataset construction method for RMS, which starts with images and end constructing paired text and guidance responses. Using the image-oriented method, we automatically generate an RMS dataset comprising 35k image-text pairs with guidance responses. Additionally, we introduce a standardized safety dataset evaluation metric: fine-tuning a safety judge model and evaluating its capabilities on other safety datasets.Extensive experiments on various tasks demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed image-oriented pipeline. The results confirm the scalability and effectiveness of the image-oriented approach, offering a new perspective for the construction of real-world multimodal safety datasets. The dataset is presented at https://huggingface.co/datasets/NewCityLetter/RMS2/tree/main."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04403","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:46:22Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:46:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:14:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04169","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04169","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04169","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Chalmers University of Technology"],"givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Johansson","name":"Johansson, Nicolas","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Chalmers University of Technology"],"givenName":"Tobias","familyName":"Olsson","name":"Olsson, Tobias","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["AI Sweden"],"givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Nilsson","name":"Nilsson, Daniel","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["AI Sweden"],"givenName":"Johan","familyName":"Östman","name":"Östman, Johan","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["AI Sweden"],"givenName":"Fazeleh","familyName":"Hoseini","name":"Hoseini, Fazeleh","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Privacy Risks in Time Series Forecasting: User- and Record-Level Membership Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T12:43:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:38:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-12T18:46:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T02:08:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Membership inference attacks (MIAs) aim to determine whether specific data were used to train a model. While extensively studied on classification models, their impact on time series forecasting remains largely unexplored. We address this gap by introducing two new attacks: (i) an adaptation of multivariate LiRA, a state-of-the-art MIA originally developed for classification models, to the time-series forecasting setting, and (ii) a novel end-to-end learning approach called Deep Time Series (DTS) attack. We benchmark these methods against adapted versions of other leading attacks from the classification setting.\n We evaluate all attacks in realistic settings on the TUH-EEG and ELD datasets, targeting two strong forecasting architectures, LSTM and the state-of-the-art N-HiTS, under both record- and user-level threat models. Our results show that forecasting models are vulnerable, with user-level attacks often achieving perfect detection. The proposed methods achieve the strongest performance in several settings, establishing new baselines for privacy risk assessment in time series forecasting. Furthermore, vulnerability increases with longer prediction horizons and smaller training populations, echoing trends observed in large language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04169","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:40:47Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:40:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:20:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04419","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04419","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04419","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingtai","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Xingtai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Zuo","name":"Zuo, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youbang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Youbang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntian","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Yuntian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhekai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuekai","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xuekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kaiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingning","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bingning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards a Unified View of Large Language Model Post-Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T17:40:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:52:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-20T05:05:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T03:00:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Two major sources of training data exist for post-training modern language models: online (model-generated rollouts) data, and offline (human or other-model demonstrations) data. These two types of data are typically used by approaches like Reinforcement Learning (RL) and Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), respectively. In this paper, we show that these approaches are not in contradiction, but are instances of a single optimization process. We derive a Unified Policy Gradient Estimator, and present the calculations of a wide spectrum of post-training approaches as the gradient of a common objective under different data distribution assumptions and various bias-variance tradeoffs. The gradient estimator is constructed with four interchangeable parts: stabilization mask, reference policy denominator, advantage estimate, and likelihood gradient. Motivated by our theoretical findings, we propose Hybrid Post-Training (HPT), an algorithm that dynamically selects different training signals. HPT is designed to yield both effective exploitation of demonstration and stable exploration without sacrificing learned reasoning patterns. We provide extensive experiments and ablation studies to verify the effectiveness of our unified theoretical framework and HPT. Across six mathematical reasoning benchmarks and two out-of-distribution suites, HPT consistently surpasses strong baselines across models of varying scales and families."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04419","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:46:46Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:46:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:03:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04448","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04448","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04448","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehong","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Zehong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wynne","familyName":"Hsu","name":"Hsu, Wynne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mong Li","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Mong Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TRUST-VL: An Explainable News Assistant for General Multimodal Misinformation Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T17:59:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:53:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T10:58:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:40:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal misinformation, encompassing textual, visual, and cross-modal distortions, poses an increasing societal threat that is amplified by generative AI. Existing methods typically focus on a single type of distortion and struggle to generalize to unseen scenarios. In this work, we observe that different distortion types share common reasoning capabilities while also requiring task-specific skills. We hypothesize that joint training across distortion types facilitates knowledge sharing and enhances the model's ability to generalize. To this end, we introduce TRUST-VL, a unified and explainable vision-language model for general multimodal misinformation detection. TRUST-VL incorporates a novel Question-Aware Visual Amplifier module, designed to extract task-specific visual features. To support training, we also construct TRUST-Instruct, a large-scale instruction dataset containing 198K samples featuring structured reasoning chains aligned with human fact-checking workflows. Extensive experiments on both in-domain and zero-shot benchmarks demonstrate that TRUST-VL achieves state-of-the-art performance, while also offering strong generalization and interpretability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Oral; Project Homepage: https://yanzehong.github.io/trust-vl/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04448","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:47:28Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:47:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-31T02:09:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.24719","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.24719","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.24719","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shravan","familyName":"Gadbail","name":"Gadbail, Shravan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masumi","familyName":"Desai","name":"Desai, Masumi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kamalakar","familyName":"Karlapalem","name":"Karlapalem, Kamalakar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Iti-Validator: A Guardrail Framework for Validating and Correcting LLM-Generated Itineraries"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T06:11:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:00:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) has enabled them to generate complex, multi-step plans and itineraries. However, these generated plans often lack temporal and spatial consistency, particularly in scenarios involving physical travel constraints. This research aims to study the temporal performance of different LLMs and presents a validation framework that evaluates and improves the temporal consistency of LLM-generated travel itineraries. The system employs multiple state-of-the-art LLMs to generate travel plans and validates them against real-world flight duration constraints using the AeroDataBox API. This work contributes to the understanding of LLM capabilities in handling complex temporal reasoning tasks like itinerary generation and provides a framework to rectify any temporal inconsistencies like overlapping journeys or unrealistic transit times in the itineraries generated by LLMs before the itinerary is given to the user. Our experiments reveal that while current LLMs frequently produce temporally inconsistent itineraries, these can be systematically and reliably corrected using our framework, enabling their practical deployment in large-scale travel planning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.24719","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-30T01:40:14Z","registered":"2025-10-30T01:40:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T01:40:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.05378","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.05378","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.05378","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Motzfeldt","name":"Motzfeldt, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joakim","familyName":"Edin","name":"Edin, Joakim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Casper L.","familyName":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Casper L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Hardmeier","name":"Hardmeier, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lars","familyName":"Maaløe","name":"Maaløe, Lars","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Rogers","name":"Rogers, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T16:31:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-09T00:02:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-01T05:16:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:29:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T05:07:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:25:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In medical coding, experts map unstructured clinical notes to alphanumeric codes for diagnoses and procedures. We introduce Code Like Humans: a new agentic framework for medical coding with large language models. It implements official coding guidelines for human experts, and it is the first solution that can support the full ICD-10 coding system (+70K labels). It achieves the best performance to date on rare diagnosis codes (fine-tuned discriminative classifiers retain an advantage for high-frequency codes, to which they are limited). Towards future work, we also contribute an analysis of system performance and identify its `blind spots' (codes that are systematically undercoded)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP Findings 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05378","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-09T02:24:08Z","registered":"2025-09-09T02:24:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:19:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04439","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04439","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04439","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Si","name":"Si, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoxiang","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Zhaoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangxu","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Fangxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiting","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianhui","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Lianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ArcMemo: Abstract Reasoning Composition with Lifelong LLM Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T17:54:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:53:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T18:25:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-09T00:04:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-04T00:01:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:15:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While inference-time scaling enables LLMs to carry out increasingly long and capable reasoning traces, the patterns and insights uncovered during these traces are immediately discarded once the context window is reset for a new query. External memory is a natural way to persist these discoveries, and recent work has shown clear benefits for reasoning-intensive tasks. We see an opportunity to make such memories more broadly reusable and scalable by moving beyond instance-based memory entries (e.g. exact query/response pairs, or summaries tightly coupled with the original problem context) toward concept-level memory: reusable, modular abstractions distilled from solution traces and stored in natural language. For future queries, relevant concepts are selectively retrieved and integrated into the prompt, enabling test-time continual learning without weight updates. Our design introduces new strategies for abstracting takeaways from rollouts and retrieving entries for new queries, promoting reuse and allowing memory to expand with additional experiences. We evaluate on ARC-AGI, a benchmark that stresses compositional generalization and abstract reasoning, making it a natural fit for concept memory. Our method yields a 7.5% relative gain over a strong no-memory baseline with performance continuing to scale with inference compute. We find abstract concepts to be the most consistent memory design, outscoring the baseline at all tested inference compute scales. Moreover, dynamically updating memory during test-time outperforms fixed settings, supporting the hypothesis that accumulating and abstracting patterns enables further solutions in a form of self-improvement. Code is available at https://github.com/matt-seb-ho/arc_memo."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04439","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:47:15Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:47:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:44:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.25198","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.25198","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.25198","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elbert","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Elbert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SOLD: SELFIES-based Objective-driven Latent Diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T18:10:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-01T00:00:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, machine learning has made a significant impact on de novo drug design. However, current approaches to creating novel molecules conditioned on a target protein typically rely on generating molecules directly in the 3D conformational space, which are often slow and overly complex. In this work, we propose SOLD (SELFIES-based Objective-driven Latent Diffusion), a novel latent diffusion model that generates molecules in a latent space derived from 1D SELFIES strings and conditioned on a target protein. In the process, we also train an innovative SELFIES transformer and propose a new way to balance losses when training multi-task machine learning models.Our model generates high-affinity molecules for the target protein in a simple and efficient way, while also leaving room for future improvements through the addition of more data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25198","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-01T02:06:31Z","registered":"2025-10-01T02:06:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-01T02:06:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04537","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04537","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04537","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryosuke","familyName":"Takata","name":"Takata, Ryosuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atsushi","familyName":"Masumori","name":"Masumori, Atsushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Ikegami","name":"Ikegami, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Emergent Social Dynamics of LLM Agents in the El Farol Bar Problem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T08:09:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-08T00:02:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-08T11:56:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-09T01:14:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T13:45:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:42:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the emergent social dynamics of Large Language Model (LLM) agents in a spatially extended El Farol Bar problem, observing how they autonomously navigate this classic social dilemma. As a result, the LLM agents generated a spontaneous motivation to go to the bar and changed their decision making by becoming a collective. We also observed that the LLM agents did not solve the problem completely, but rather behaved more like humans. These findings reveal a complex interplay between external incentives (prompt-specified constraints such as the 60% threshold) and internal incentives (culturally-encoded social preferences derived from pre-training), demonstrating that LLM agents naturally balance formal game-theoretic rationality with social motivations that characterize human behavior. These findings suggest that a new model of group decision making, which could not be handled in the previous game-theoretic problem setting, can be realized by LLM agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04537","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-08T01:27:18Z","registered":"2025-09-08T01:27:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:48:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12221","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12221","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12221","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingya","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T07:16:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:00:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) enforce safety alignment to reliably refuse malicious requests, yet the same blanket safeguards also block legitimate uses in policing, defense, and other high-stakes settings. Earlier \"refusal-direction\" edits can bypass those layers, but they rely on a single vector that indiscriminately unlocks all hazardous topics, offering no semantic control. We introduce Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors (MEUV), a lightweight framework that factorizes the monolithic refusal direction into topic-aligned, nearly orthogonal vectors, each dedicated to one sensitive capability. MEUV is learned in a single epoch with a multi-task objective that blends a differential-ablation margin, cross-topic and orthogonality penalties, and several auxiliary terms. On bilingual malicious-prompt benchmarks, MEUV achieves an attack success rate of no less than 87% on Gemma-2-2B, LLaMA-3-8B, and Qwen-7B, yet cuts cross-topic leakage by up to 90% compared with the best single-direction baseline. Vectors trained in Chinese transfer almost unchanged to English (and vice versa), suggesting a language-agnostic refusal subspace. The results show that fine-grained, topic-level capability activation is achievable with minimal utility loss, paving the way for controlled LLMs deployment in security-sensitive domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12221","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:35:29Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:35:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:35:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10526","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10526","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10526","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dieter","familyName":"Balemans","name":"Balemans, Dieter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Huybrechts","name":"Huybrechts, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Steckel","name":"Steckel, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siegfried","familyName":"Mercelis","name":"Mercelis, Siegfried","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Resource-Aware Neural Network Pruning Using Graph-based Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T15:05:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:01:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a novel approach to neural network pruning by integrating a graph-based observation space into an AutoML framework to address the limitations of existing methods. Traditional pruning approaches often depend on hand-crafted heuristics and local optimization perspectives, which can lead to suboptimal performance and inefficient pruning strategies. Our framework transforms the pruning process by introducing a graph representation of the target neural network that captures complete topological relationships between layers and channels, replacing the limited layer-wise observation space with a global view of network structure. The core innovations include a Graph Attention Network (GAT) encoder that processes the network's graph representation and generates a rich embedding. Additionally, for the action space we transition from continuous pruning ratios to fine-grained binary action spaces which enables the agent to learn optimal channel importance criteria directly from data, moving away from predefined scoring functions. These contributions are modelled within a Constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP) framework, allowing the agent to make informed pruning decisions while adhering to resource constraints such as target compression rates. For this, we design a self-competition reward system that encourages the agent to outperform its previous best performance while satisfying the defined constraints. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach through extensive experiments on benchmark datasets including CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and ImageNet. The experiments show that our method consistently outperforms traditional pruning techniques, showing state-of-the-art results while learning task-specific pruning strategies that identify functionally redundant connections beyond simple weight magnitude considerations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10526","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:41:20Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:41:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:41:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04373","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04373","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04373","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bufan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Bufan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elisa","familyName":"Kreiss","name":"Kreiss, Elisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Measuring Bias or Measuring the Task: Understanding the Brittle Nature of LLM Gender Biases"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T16:32:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:50:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-08T22:42:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:11:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-10T06:08:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-11T00:18:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As LLMs are increasingly applied in socially impactful settings, concerns about gender bias have prompted growing efforts both to measure and mitigate such bias. These efforts often rely on evaluation tasks that differ from natural language distributions, as they typically involve carefully constructed task prompts that overtly or covertly signal the presence of gender bias-related content. In this paper, we examine how signaling the evaluative purpose of a task impacts measured gender bias in LLMs. Concretely, we test models under prompt conditions that (1) make the testing context salient, and (2) make gender-focused content salient. We then assess prompt sensitivity across four task formats with both token-probability and discrete-choice metrics. We find that prompts that more clearly align with (gender bias) evaluation framing elicit distinct gender output distributions compared to less evaluation-framed prompts. Discrete-choice metrics further tend to amplify bias relative to probabilistic measures. These findings do not only highlight the brittleness of LLM gender bias evaluations but open a new puzzle for the NLP benchmarking and development community: To what extent can well-controlled testing designs trigger LLM \"testing mode\" performance, and what does this mean for the ecological validity of future benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To be published at EMNLP 2025 (main conference)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04373","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:45:39Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:45:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-11T01:51:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.05360","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.05360","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.05360","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerry","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jerry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evangelos","familyName":"Papalexakis","name":"Papalexakis, Evangelos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond ROUGE: N-Gram Subspace Features for LLM Hallucination Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T18:52:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-09T00:01:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated effectiveness across a wide variety of tasks involving natural language, however, a fundamental problem of hallucinations still plagues these models, limiting their trustworthiness in generating consistent, truthful information. Detecting hallucinations has quickly become an important topic, with various methods such as uncertainty estimation, LLM Judges, retrieval augmented generation (RAG), and consistency checks showing promise. Many of these methods build upon foundational metrics, such as ROUGE, BERTScore, or Perplexity, which often lack the semantic depth necessary to detect hallucinations effectively. In this work, we propose a novel approach inspired by ROUGE that constructs an N-Gram frequency tensor from LLM-generated text. This tensor captures richer semantic structure by encoding co-occurrence patterns, enabling better differentiation between factual and hallucinated content. We demonstrate this by applying tensor decomposition methods to extract singular values from each mode and use these as input features to train a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) binary classifier for hallucinations. Our method is evaluated on the HaluEval dataset and demonstrates significant improvements over traditional baselines, as well as competitive performance against state-of-the-art LLM judges."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05360","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-09T02:23:42Z","registered":"2025-09-09T02:23:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-09T02:23:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04534","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04534","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04534","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaifu","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Zaifu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meijia","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Meijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantized Large Language Models in Biomedical Natural Language Processing: Evaluation and Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T04:18:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-08T00:02:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in biomedical natural language processing, yet their rapid growth in size and computational requirements present a major barrier to adoption in healthcare settings where data privacy precludes cloud deployment and resources are limited. In this study, we systematically evaluated the impact of quantization on 12 state-of-the-art large language models, including both general-purpose and biomedical-specific models, across eight benchmark datasets covering four key tasks: named entity recognition, relation extraction, multi-label classification, and question answering. We show that quantization substantially reduces GPU memory requirements-by up to 75%-while preserving model performance across diverse tasks, enabling the deployment of 70B-parameter models on 40GB consumer-grade GPUs. In addition, domain-specific knowledge and responsiveness to advanced prompting methods are largely maintained. These findings provide significant practical and guiding value, highlighting quantization as a practical and effective strategy for enabling the secure, local deployment of large yet high-capacity language models in biomedical contexts, bridging the gap between technical advances in AI and real-world clinical translation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04534","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-08T01:27:14Z","registered":"2025-09-08T01:27:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-08T01:27:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04442","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04442","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04442","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqiu","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhiqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amish","familyName":"Sethi","name":"Sethi, Amish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mayur","familyName":"Naik","name":"Naik, Mayur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ser-Nam","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Ser-Nam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Delta Activations: A Representation for Finetuned Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T17:59:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:53:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The success of powerful open source Large Language Models (LLMs) has enabled the community to create a vast collection of post-trained models adapted to specific tasks and domains. However, navigating and understanding these models remains challenging due to inconsistent metadata and unstructured repositories. We introduce Delta Activations, a method to represent finetuned models as vector embeddings by measuring shifts in their internal activations relative to a base model. This representation allows for effective clustering by domain and task, revealing structure in the model landscape. Delta Activations also demonstrate desirable properties: it is robust across finetuning settings and exhibits an additive property when finetuning datasets are mixed. In addition, we show that Delta Activations can embed tasks via few-shot finetuning, and further explore its use for model selection and merging. We hope Delta Activations can facilitate the practice of reusing publicly available models. Code is available at https://github.com/OscarXZQ/delta_activations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04442","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:47:20Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:47:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-05T01:47:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04377","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04377","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04377","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krishna Teja","familyName":"Chitty-Venkata","name":"Chitty-Venkata, Krishna Teja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xian-He","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xian-He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony","familyName":"Kougkas","name":"Kougkas, Anthony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murali","familyName":"Emani","name":"Emani, Murali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Venkatram","familyName":"Vishwanath","name":"Vishwanath, Venkatram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bogdan","familyName":"Nicolae","name":"Nicolae, Bogdan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PagedEviction: Structured Block-wise KV Cache Pruning for Efficient Large Language Model Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T16:40:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:50:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"KV caching significantly improves the efficiency of Large Language Model (LLM) inference by storing attention states from previously processed tokens, enabling faster generation of subsequent tokens. However, as sequence length increases, the KV cache quickly becomes a major memory bottleneck. To address this, we propose PagedEviction, a novel fine-grained, structured KV cache pruning strategy that enhances the memory efficiency of vLLM's PagedAttention. Unlike existing approaches that rely on attention-based token importance or evict tokens across different vLLM pages, PagedEviction introduces an efficient block-wise eviction algorithm tailored for paged memory layouts. Our method integrates seamlessly with PagedAttention without requiring any modifications to its CUDA attention kernels. We evaluate PagedEviction across Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct, and Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct models on the LongBench benchmark suite, demonstrating improved memory usage with better accuracy than baselines on long context tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04377","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:45:44Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:45:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-05T01:45:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04343","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04343","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04343","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maciej","familyName":"Besta","name":"Besta, Maciej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shriram","familyName":"Chandran","name":"Chandran, Shriram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Gerstenberger","name":"Gerstenberger, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathis","familyName":"Lindner","name":"Lindner, Mathis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcin","familyName":"Chrapek","name":"Chrapek, Marcin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian Hermann","familyName":"Martschat","name":"Martschat, Sebastian Hermann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taraneh","familyName":"Ghandi","name":"Ghandi, Taraneh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Iff","name":"Iff, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hubert","familyName":"Niewiadomski","name":"Niewiadomski, Hubert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piotr","familyName":"Nyczyk","name":"Nyczyk, Piotr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jürgen","familyName":"Müller","name":"Müller, Jürgen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Torsten","familyName":"Hoefler","name":"Hoefler, Torsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Psychologically Enhanced AI Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T16:03:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:48:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce MBTI-in-Thoughts, a framework for enhancing the effectiveness of Large Language Model (LLM) agents through psychologically grounded personality conditioning. Drawing on the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI), our method primes agents with distinct personality archetypes via prompt engineering, enabling control over behavior along two foundational axes of human psychology, cognition and affect. We show that such personality priming yields consistent, interpretable behavioral biases across diverse tasks: emotionally expressive agents excel in narrative generation, while analytically primed agents adopt more stable strategies in game-theoretic settings. Our framework supports experimenting with structured multi-agent communication protocols and reveals that self-reflection prior to interaction improves cooperation and reasoning quality. To ensure trait persistence, we integrate the official 16Personalities test for automated verification. While our focus is on MBTI, we show that our approach generalizes seamlessly to other psychological frameworks such as Big Five, HEXACO, or Enneagram. By bridging psychological theory and LLM behavior design, we establish a foundation for psychologically enhanced AI agents without any fine-tuning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04343","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:44:55Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:44:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-05T01:44:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04324","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04324","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04324","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shan","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Shan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Letizia","familyName":"Gionfrida","name":"Gionfrida, Letizia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OVGrasp: Open-Vocabulary Grasping Assistance via Multimodal Intent Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T15:42:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:48:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Grasping assistance is essential for restoring autonomy in individuals with motor impairments, particularly in unstructured environments where object categories and user intentions are diverse and unpredictable. We present OVGrasp, a hierarchical control framework for soft exoskeleton-based grasp assistance that integrates RGB-D vision, open-vocabulary prompts, and voice commands to enable robust multimodal interaction. To enhance generalization in open environments, OVGrasp incorporates a vision-language foundation model with an open-vocabulary mechanism, allowing zero-shot detection of previously unseen objects without retraining. A multimodal decision-maker further fuses spatial and linguistic cues to infer user intent, such as grasp or release, in multi-object scenarios. We deploy the complete framework on a custom egocentric-view wearable exoskeleton and conduct systematic evaluations on 15 objects across three grasp types. Experimental results with ten participants demonstrate that OVGrasp achieves a grasping ability score (GAS) of 87.00%, outperforming state-of-the-art baselines and achieving improved kinematic alignment with natural hand motion."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04324","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:44:28Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:44:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-05T01:44:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04304","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04304","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04304","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juraj","familyName":"Vladika","name":"Vladika, Juraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdi","familyName":"Dhaini","name":"Dhaini, Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Matthes","name":"Matthes, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Facts Fade Fast: Evaluating Memorization of Outdated Medical Knowledge in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T15:17:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:46:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The growing capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) show significant potential to enhance healthcare by assisting medical researchers and physicians. However, their reliance on static training data is a major risk when medical recommendations evolve with new research and developments. When LLMs memorize outdated medical knowledge, they can provide harmful advice or fail at clinical reasoning tasks. To investigate this problem, we introduce two novel question-answering (QA) datasets derived from systematic reviews: MedRevQA (16,501 QA pairs covering general biomedical knowledge) and MedChangeQA (a subset of 512 QA pairs where medical consensus has changed over time). Our evaluation of eight prominent LLMs on the datasets reveals consistent reliance on outdated knowledge across all models. We additionally analyze the influence of obsolete pre-training data and training strategies to explain this phenomenon and propose future directions for mitigation, laying the groundwork for developing more current and reliable medical AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Findings of EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04304","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:44:00Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:44:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-05T01:44:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04292","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04292","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04292","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinyan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qinyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinping","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Xinping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruijie","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Ruijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haojie","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Haojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiafan","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Jiafan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingling","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dingling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongfei","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Zhongfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqiang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ziqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changxin","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Changxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingkai","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jingkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mengyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiaheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaiyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zaiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inverse IFEval: Can LLMs Unlearn Stubborn Training Conventions to Follow Real Instructions?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T15:03:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:45:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) achieve strong performance on diverse tasks but often exhibit cognitive inertia, struggling to follow instructions that conflict with the standardized patterns learned during supervised fine-tuning (SFT). To evaluate this limitation, we propose Inverse IFEval, a benchmark that measures models Counter-intuitive Abilitytheir capacity to override training-induced biases and comply with adversarial instructions. Inverse IFEval introduces eight types of such challenges, including Question Correction, Intentional Textual Flaws, Code without Comments, and Counterfactual Answering. Using a human-in-the-loop pipeline, we construct a dataset of 1012 high-quality Chinese and English questions across 23 domains, evaluated under an optimized LLM-as-a-Judge framework. Experiments on existing leading LLMs demonstrate the necessity of our proposed Inverse IFEval benchmark. Our findings emphasize that future alignment efforts should not only pursue fluency and factual correctness but also account for adaptability under unconventional contexts. We hope that Inverse IFEval serves as both a diagnostic tool and a foundation for developing methods that mitigate cognitive inertia, reduce overfitting to narrow patterns, and ultimately enhance the instruction-following reliability of LLMs in diverse and unpredictable real-world scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04292","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:43:42Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:43:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-05T01:43:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04243","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04243","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04243","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanfu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wanfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qipeng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Qipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangquan","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Guangquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobo","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Xiaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juntao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Active Perception via Self-Evolving Preference Optimization for GUI Grounding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T14:17:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:43:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision Language Models (VLMs) have recently achieved significant progress in bridging visual perception and linguistic reasoning. Recently, OpenAI o3 model introduced a zoom-in search strategy that effectively elicits active perception capabilities in VLMs, improving downstream task performance. However, enabling VLMs to reason effectively over appropriate image regions remains a core challenge in GUI grounding, particularly under high-resolution inputs and complex multi-element visual interactions. In this work, we propose LASER, a self-evolving framework that progressively endows VLMs with multi-step perception capabilities, enabling precise coordinate prediction. Specifically, our approach integrate Monte Carlo quality estimation with Intersection-over-Union (IoU)-based region quality evaluation to jointly encourage both accuracy and diversity in constructing high-quality preference data. This combination explicitly guides the model to focus on instruction-relevant key regions while adaptively allocating reasoning steps based on task complexity. Comprehensive experiments on the ScreenSpot Pro and ScreenSpot-v2 benchmarks demonstrate consistent performance gains, validating the effectiveness of our method. Furthermore, when fine-tuned on GTA1-7B, LASER achieves a score of 55.7 on the ScreenSpot-Pro benchmark, establishing a new state-of-the-art (SoTA) among 7B-scale models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04243","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:42:32Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:42:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-05T01:42:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04213","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04213","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04213","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobin","familyName":"Holtmann","name":"Holtmann, Tobin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Stenger","name":"Stenger, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andres","familyName":"Posada-Moreno","name":"Posada-Moreno, Andres","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Friedrich","familyName":"Solowjow","name":"Solowjow, Friedrich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Trimpe","name":"Trimpe, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sailing Towards Zero-Shot State Estimation using Foundation Models Combined with a UKF"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T13:38:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:41:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"State estimation in control and systems engineering traditionally requires extensive manual system identification or data-collection effort. However, transformer-based foundation models in other domains have reduced data requirements by leveraging pre-trained generalist models. Ultimately, developing zero-shot foundation models of system dynamics could drastically reduce manual deployment effort. While recent work shows that transformer-based end-to-end approaches can achieve zero-shot performance on unseen systems, they are limited to sensor models seen during training. We introduce the foundation model unscented Kalman filter (FM-UKF), which combines a transformer-based model of system dynamics with analytically known sensor models via an UKF, enabling generalization across varying dynamics without retraining for new sensor configurations. We evaluate FM-UKF on a new benchmark of container ship models with complex dynamics, demonstrating a competitive accuracy, effort, and robustness trade-off compared to classical methods with approximate system knowledge and to an end-to-end approach. The benchmark and dataset are open sourced to further support future research in zero-shot state estimation via foundation models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication at CDC2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04213","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:41:49Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:41:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-05T01:41:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04198","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04198","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04198","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Petr","familyName":"Průcha","name":"Průcha, Petr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michaela","familyName":"Matoušková","name":"Matoušková, Michaela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Strnad","name":"Strnad, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are LLM Agents the New RPA? A Comparative Study with RPA Across Enterprise Workflows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T13:22:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:40:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The emergence of large language models (LLMs) has introduced a new paradigm in automation: LLM agents or Agentic Automation with Computer Use (AACU). Unlike traditional Robotic Process Automation (RPA), which relies on rule-based workflows and scripting, AACU enables intelligent agents to perform tasks through natural language instructions and autonomous interaction with user interfaces. This study investigates whether AACU can serve as a viable alternative to RPA in enterprise workflow automation. We conducted controlled experiments across three standard RPA challenges data entry, monitoring, and document extraction comparing RPA (via UiPath) and AACU (via Anthropic's Computer Use Agent) in terms of speed, reliability, and development effort. Results indicate that RPA outperforms AACU in execution speed and reliability, particularly in repetitive, stable environments. However, AACU significantly reduces development time and adapts more flexibly to dynamic interfaces. While current AACU implementations are not yet production-ready, their promise in rapid prototyping and lightweight automation is evident. Future research should explore multi-agent orchestration, hybrid RPA-AACU architectures, and more robust evaluation across industries and platforms."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04198","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:41:28Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:41:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-05T01:41:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04185","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04185","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04185","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Itai","familyName":"Gat","name":"Gat, Itai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heli","familyName":"Ben-Hamu","name":"Ben-Hamu, Heli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marton","familyName":"Havasi","name":"Havasi, Marton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Haziza","name":"Haziza, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremy","familyName":"Reizenstein","name":"Reizenstein, Jeremy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Synnaeve","name":"Synnaeve, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Lopez-Paz","name":"Lopez-Paz, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Karrer","name":"Karrer, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaron","familyName":"Lipman","name":"Lipman, Yaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Set Block Decoding is a Language Model Inference Accelerator"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T13:02:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:39:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autoregressive next token prediction language models offer powerful capabilities but face significant challenges in practical deployment due to the high computational and memory costs of inference, particularly during the decoding stage. We introduce Set Block Decoding (SBD), a simple and flexible paradigm that accelerates generation by integrating standard next token prediction (NTP) and masked token prediction (MATP) within a single architecture. SBD allows the model to sample multiple, not necessarily consecutive, future tokens in parallel, a key distinction from previous acceleration methods. This flexibility allows the use of advanced solvers from the discrete diffusion literature, offering significant speedups without sacrificing accuracy. SBD requires no architectural changes or extra training hyperparameters, maintains compatibility with exact KV-caching, and can be implemented by fine-tuning existing next token prediction models. By fine-tuning Llama-3.1 8B and Qwen-3 8B, we demonstrate that SBD enables a 3-5x reduction in the number of forward passes required for generation while achieving same performance as equivalent NTP training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04185","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:41:10Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:41:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-05T01:41:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":30,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=%28doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04169+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04183+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04185+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04198+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04213+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04243+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04292+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04304+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04310+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04324+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04334+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04343+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04373+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04377+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04403+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04419+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04439+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04442+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04448+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04534+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04537+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.05360+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.05367+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.05378+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.09700+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10526+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12221+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.19305+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.25198+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.24719%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=30"}}